data.content:
## Breaking Change in EF Core 11 (Cosmos DB Provider)

In .NET 11, the **Cosmos DB provider removes synchronous API support entirely**. The sync-over-async opt-in (`CosmosOptionsExtension` / `EnableSyncOverAsync`) that you configured in .NET 10 no longer exists. All synchronous LINQ operators and `SaveChanges()` now throw **`InvalidOperationException`** at runtime.

**Every method in your repository breaks:**

| Sync (breaks) | Async (replacement) |
|---|---|
| `.ToList()` | `.ToListAsync()` |
| `.First(...)` | `.FirstAsync(...)` |
| `.Count()` | `.CountAsync()` |
| `.SaveChanges()` | `.SaveChangesAsync()` |

### Fixed Repository

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key points:**
- This only affects the **Cosmos DB provider** — the relational providers still support sync APIs.
- Callers must be updated to `await` these methods, so the async change propagates up the call stack.
- `Add()` remains synchronous (it's an in-memory tracking operation, not a database call).
